# E2E Testing Rules

## Discover project tooling first

Identify the E2E framework from `package.json` and config files (e.g., `playwright.config.*`, `cypress.config.*`, `wdio.conf.*`). Use the project's existing utilities, fixtures, helpers, and page-object conventions — do not introduce a parallel setup.

---

## Core principles

### Use Page Object Model

Every E2E test must interact with the UI through page objects — never write selectors directly in test specs. Page objects encapsulate selectors and interaction logic; tests express intent.

### Never use arbitrary timeouts

Do not use `waitForTimeout`, `sleep`, or equivalent arbitrary pauses. Always wait for a specific element state (visible, enabled, detached) or a network/load event. Flaky tests almost always trace back to hardcoded waits.

### Set up state via API or DB — never via UI

Create test data before the test starts, using API calls, database helpers, or seed scripts. UI-driven setup is slow, brittle, and conflates two test surfaces. Tear down or reset state between tests so they remain independent.

### Use stable selectors

Prefer explicit test identifiers (`data-testid`, `data-cy`, `aria-label`, or role-based queries) over CSS class names, DOM position, or visual text that changes with copy updates. Stable selectors survive styling refactors.

### Test from the user's perspective

Tests should exercise the application the way a real user would — navigate, interact, assert on visible outcomes. Do not assert on internal state, component props, or implementation details.

### Isolate test data

Each test must be able to run independently in any order. Tests must not share mutable state. Reset or recreate the required state at the start of each test.

---

## Anti-patterns

- Arbitrary `waitForTimeout` / `sleep` calls.
- Selectors based on CSS class names or DOM indices.
- Creating test data through the UI instead of via API/DB.
- Tests that depend on the outcome of a previous test.
- Asserting on implementation details instead of user-visible behavior.
